Now we'll see how logging works directly inside a Spring Boot application, including the easiest way to create a logger using:
private static final Logger logger
and then using Lombok's @Slf4j to avoid writing that code manually.


5. Using Logging in a Spring Boot Application
There are two common ways to create and use a logger.

Method 1: Using Logger and LoggerFactory
Example in a Spring Boot service:
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class CustomerService {

    private static final Logger logger =
            LoggerFactory.getLogger(CustomerService.class);

    public void createCustomer() {

        logger.info("Customer creation started");

        // business logic

        logger.info("Customer created successfully");
    }
}
Here:
private static final Logger logger =
        LoggerFactory.getLogger(CustomerService.class);
creates a logger for the CustomerService class.
Then we can use:
logger.info("Message");

logger.debug("Message");

logger.warn("Message");

logger.error("Message");

Method 2: Using Lombok @Slf4j
Instead of writing:
private static final Logger logger =
        LoggerFactory.getLogger(CustomerService.class);
Lombok can generate it for us.
import lombok.extern.slf4j.Slf4j;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Slf4j
@Service
public class CustomerService {

    public void createCustomer() {

        log.info("Customer creation started");

        // business logic

        log.info("Customer created successfully");
    }
}
The annotation:
@Slf4j
automatically creates a logger named:
log
So we can directly write:
log.info("Customer created");

log.debug("Debugging customer");

log.warn("Something unusual happened");

log.error("Customer creation failed");

What does Lombok generate internally?
Conceptually, this:
@Slf4j
public class CustomerService {
}
is similar to writing:
private static final Logger log =
        LoggerFactory.getLogger(CustomerService.class);
So:
@Slf4j saves us from manually creating the Logger object.

Which one should you use?
Without Lombok
private static final Logger logger =
        LoggerFactory.getLogger(CustomerService.class);
Use:
logger.info("Customer created");
With Lombok
@Slf4j
Use:
log.info("Customer created");
In modern Spring Boot projects, @Slf4j is commonly used because it reduces boilerplate.

One important point
You can use logging in any layer:
Controller
    ↓
Service
    ↓
Repository
For example:
@Slf4j
@RestController
public class CustomerController {

    @PostMapping("/customers")
    public String createCustomer() {

        log.info("Received request to create customer");

        return "Customer created";
    }
}
The console may show something like:
INFO  CustomerController - Received request to create customer

Simple summary
SLF4J
  ↓
Logger
  ↓
log.info()
log.debug()
log.warn()
log.error()
  ↓
Logback
  ↓
Console / File
