How Spring Boot decides which log messages to display using log levels and application.properties.
This is where you'll understand why sometimes:
log.debug("Hello");
does not appear in the console.
6. Configuring Log Levels in Spring Boot
Now let's understand why some logs appear and some do not.
We have:
log.trace("Trace message");
log.debug("Debug message");
log.info("Info message");
log.warn("Warning message");
log.error("Error message");
The log levels are:
TRACE
DEBUG
INFO
WARN
ERROR
They go from lowest level to highest severity:
------------------------------------------------
TRACE → DEBUG → INFO → WARN → ERROR
------------------------------------------

Default behavior in Spring Boot
By default, Spring Boot generally shows logs from:
INFO
WARN
ERROR
So if you write:
@Slf4j
@Service
public class CustomerService {

    public void createCustomer() {

        log.trace("TRACE message");

        log.debug("DEBUG message");

        log.info("INFO message");

        log.warn("WARN message");

        log.error("ERROR message");
    }
}
You will normally see:
INFO  - INFO message
WARN  - WARN message
ERROR - ERROR message
But not:
TRACE
DEBUG
Why?
Because the default log level is typically:
INFO

How does the level work?
Suppose the configured level is:

logging.level.root=INFO

Then Spring Boot displays:
INFO
WARN
ERROR
But hides:
DEBUG
TRACE
Visual representation
TRACE   ❌
DEBUG   ❌
INFO    ✅
WARN    ✅
ERROR   ✅

Enable DEBUG logs
Open:
application.properties
Add:

logging.level.root=DEBUG

Now Spring Boot can display:
DEBUG
INFO
WARN
ERROR
TRACE   ❌
DEBUG   ✅
INFO    ✅
WARN    ✅
ERROR   ✅

Enable TRACE logs

logging.level.root=TRACE

Now everything is displayed:
TRACE   ✅
DEBUG   ✅
INFO    ✅
WARN    ✅
ERROR   ✅

Set logging level for your own package
Usually, you don't want to enable DEBUG for the entire Spring Boot application.
For example, suppose your package is:
com.example.cruds
You can write:
logging.level.com.example.cruds=DEBUG
Now DEBUG logs are enabled only for your application package.
Example:
package com.example.cruds.service;

@Slf4j
@Service
public class CustomerService {

    public void getCustomer(Long id) {

        log.debug("Fetching customer with ID: {}", id);

        log.info("Customer fetched successfully");
    }
}
You may see:
DEBUG ... CustomerService - Fetching customer with ID: 10
INFO  ... CustomerService - Customer fetched successfully

Why configure packages separately?
Suppose you do this:
logging.level.root=DEBUG
Then you may get a huge amount of logs from Spring itself:
Spring
Hibernate
Tomcat
Security
JPA
Your console can become messy.
Instead, you can keep the root level at INFO:
logging.level.root=INFO
And enable DEBUG only for your code:
logging.level.com.example.cruds=DEBUG
So:
Spring Boot logs     → INFO
Your application     → DEBUG
This is the commonly useful approach.

Example configuration
logging.level.root=INFO

logging.level.com.example.cruds=DEBUG
Then in your service:
@Slf4j
@Service
public class CustomerService {

    public void createCustomer(Customer customer) {

        log.debug("Received customer: {}", customer);

        log.info("Creating customer");

        try {

            // save customer

            log.info("Customer created successfully");

        } catch (Exception e) {

            log.error("Failed to create customer", e);
        }
    }
}
Notice this:
log.debug("Received customer: {}", customer);
We use {} instead of:
log.debug("Received customer: " + customer);
We'll discuss why {} is preferred shortly.
Important takeaway
The configured log level acts like a minimum level to display.
For example:
logging.level.com.example.cruds=WARN
Then:
TRACE   ❌
DEBUG   ❌
INFO    ❌
WARN    ✅
ERROR   ✅

Next topic
Logging variables and objects using {} placeholders, and why this is better than string concatenation:
log.info("Customer ID: {}", customerId);
instead of:
log.info("Customer ID: " + customerId);

7. Logging Variables Using {}
Suppose you want to log a customer ID.
You might write:
log.info("Customer ID: " + customerId);
But with SLF4J, the preferred way is:
log.info("Customer ID: {}", customerId);
Here:
{}
is a placeholder.
The value of customerId is inserted into {}.
For example:
Long customerId = 10L;

log.info("Fetching customer with ID: {}", customerId);
Output:
Fetching customer with ID: 10

Multiple variables
You can use multiple placeholders:
log.info(
    "Creating customer with name: {} and email: {}",
    customer.getName(),
    customer.getEmail()
);
Output:
Creating customer with name: Ram and email: ram@gmail.com
Each value is inserted in order:
First {}  → customer.getName()

Second {} → customer.getEmail()

Why is {} better than string concatenation?
String concatenation
log.debug("Customer ID: " + customerId);
Even if the DEBUG log level is disabled, Java may still construct the string.
Placeholder
log.debug("Customer ID: {}", customerId);
The logging framework can avoid unnecessary message formatting when that log level is disabled.
So the preferred approach is:
log.info("Customer ID: {}", customerId);
instead of:
log.info("Customer ID: " + customerId);

Logging objects
Suppose:
Customer customer = new Customer();
You can log the object:
log.debug("Customer details: {}", customer);
SLF4J will internally use the object's toString() representation.
For example, if:
@Override
public String toString() {
    return "Customer{name='" + name + "', email='" + email + "'}";
}
Then:
Customer details: Customer{name='Ram', email='ram@gmail.com'}
If you use Lombok's:
@Data
or:
@ToString
a toString() method can be generated automatically.

Exception logging
Suppose an exception occurs:
try {

    customerRepository.save(customer);

} catch (Exception e) {

    log.error("Failed to save customer");
}
This only logs:
Failed to save customer
You don't see the actual exception details.
Instead:
catch (Exception e) {

    log.error("Failed to save customer", e);
}
Now you get:
Failed to save customer
along with the exception and stack trace.
You can also include variables:
catch (Exception e) {

    log.error(
        "Failed to save customer with ID: {}",
        customer.getId(),
        e
    );
}
This logs:
Failed to save customer with ID: 10
plus the stack trace.

Simple rule to remember
log.info("Message");

log.info("Customer ID: {}", customerId);

log.info(
    "Customer: {}, Email: {}",
    name,
    email
);

log.error("Something went wrong", exception);
Important
Avoid logging sensitive information such as:
Passwords
Tokens
Credit card details
Secrets
Even in debugging logs.
8. Where to Place Logs in a Real Spring Boot Application
In a typical application:
Client
   ↓
Controller
   ↓
Service
   ↓
Repository
   ↓
Database
You do not need to log everything everywhere. Log meaningful events.

1. Controller Layer
The Controller receives the HTTP request.
Example:
@Slf4j
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/customers")
public class CustomerController {

    @PostMapping
    public CustomerResponseDTO createCustomer(
            @RequestBody CustomerRequestDTO request) {

        log.info("Received request to create customer");

        return customerService.createCustomer(request);
    }
}
Here, we log:
A request has reached the application.
You can also log important request details:
log.info(
    "Received request to fetch customer with ID: {}",
    customerId
);

2. Service Layer
The Service contains the business logic.
This is usually where most useful application logs are written.
@Slf4j
@Service
public class CustomerService {

    public CustomerResponseDTO createCustomer(
            CustomerRequestDTO request) {

        log.info(
            "Starting customer creation for email: {}",
            request.getEmail()
        );

        // business logic

        log.info("Customer created successfully");

        return response;
    }
}
A good flow is:
INFO → Operation started

DEBUG → Important internal details

INFO → Operation completed

ERROR → Operation failed
Example:
public Customer getCustomer(Long id) {

    log.info("Fetching customer with ID: {}", id);

    Customer customer = customerRepository
            .findById(id)
            .orElseThrow(() -> {

                log.warn(
                    "Customer not found with ID: {}",
                    id
                );

                return new ResourceNotFoundException(
                    "Customer not found"
                );
            });

    log.debug("Customer found: {}", customer);

    return customer;
}

3. Repository Layer
The Repository communicates with the database.
Usually, you don't need to manually log every repository call.
For example:
customerRepository.save(customer);
You don't necessarily need:
log.info("Calling repository save method");
Why?
Because it can create unnecessary logs.
Instead, log the important business operation in the service:
log.info("Saving customer with email: {}", email);

customerRepository.save(customer);
If you want to see generated SQL, Spring Boot/Hibernate can log it separately.

Complete Example
Let's see the full flow.
Controller
@Slf4j
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/customers")
public class CustomerController {

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public Customer getCustomer(
            @PathVariable Long id) {

        log.info(
            "Received request for customer ID: {}",
            id
        );

        return customerService.getCustomer(id);
    }
}
Service
@Slf4j
@Service
public class CustomerService {

    public Customer getCustomer(Long id) {

        log.info(
            "Fetching customer with ID: {}",
            id
        );

        Customer customer = customerRepository
                .findById(id)
                .orElseThrow(() -> {

                    log.warn(
                        "Customer not found with ID: {}",
                        id
                    );

                    return new ResourceNotFoundException(
                            "Customer not found"
                    );
                });

        log.info(
            "Customer fetched successfully with ID: {}",
            id
        );

        return customer;
    }
}

What happens when the API is called?
Suppose the client calls:
GET /customers/10
The logs could be:
INFO  CustomerController - Received request for customer ID: 10

INFO  CustomerService - Fetching customer with ID: 10

INFO  CustomerService - Customer fetched successfully with ID: 10
If the customer does not exist:
INFO  CustomerController - Received request for customer ID: 10

INFO  CustomerService - Fetching customer with ID: 10

WARN  CustomerService - Customer not found with ID: 10
If something unexpected happens:
catch (Exception e) {

    log.error(
        "Unexpected error while fetching customer with ID: {}",
        id,
        e
    );

    throw e;
}

Simple Logging Strategy
For your Spring Boot applications, initially follow this:
Layer
What to log
Controller
Important incoming requests
Service
Start/end of important business operations
Service
Important IDs or debugging details
Service
Warnings and errors
Repository
Usually no manual logs needed

Simple rule
Log the important flow of the application, not every single line of code.
9. Logging Exceptions and Global Exception Handling
Now let's connect logging with something you already learned:
Exception
   ↓
Global Exception Handler
   ↓
Error Response
The important question is:
Where should we log the exception?

Example flow
Suppose we have this service:
@Slf4j
@Service
public class CustomerService {

    public Customer getCustomer(Long id) {

        return customerRepository.findById(id)
                .orElseThrow(() -> {
                    
                    log.warn(
                        "Customer not found with ID: {}",
                        id
                    );

                    return new ResourceNotFoundException(
                        "Customer not found with ID: " + id
                    );
                });
    }
}
If customer 10 does not exist:
GET /customers/10
The flow is:
Customer not found
        ↓
ResourceNotFoundException thrown
        ↓
GlobalExceptionHandler catches it
        ↓
Error response returned to client

Should we log again in the Global Exception Handler?
You can, but avoid unnecessarily logging the same exception multiple times.
For an expected exception such as:
Customer not found
Invalid input
Duplicate email
you might log it as WARN.
Example:
@Slf4j
@RestControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(ResourceNotFoundException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ErrorResponse> handleNotFound(
            ResourceNotFoundException ex) {

        log.warn("Resource not found: {}", ex.getMessage());

        ErrorResponse response = new ErrorResponse(
                HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND.value(),
                ex.getMessage()
        );

        return ResponseEntity
                .status(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)
                .body(response);
    }
}

Unexpected exceptions
Now suppose something unexpected happens:
Database connection failure
NullPointerException
Unexpected server error
These are good candidates for ERROR logging.
@Slf4j
@RestControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ErrorResponse> handleException(
            Exception ex) {

        log.error(
                "Unexpected error occurred",
                ex
        );

        ErrorResponse response = new ErrorResponse(
                HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR.value(),
                "Something went wrong"
        );

        return ResponseEntity
                .status(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR)
                .body(response);
    }
}
Notice:
log.error("Unexpected error occurred", ex);
Passing ex as the last argument logs the full stack trace.

Important: ex.getMessage() vs ex
Only message
log.error("Error: {}", ex.getMessage());
You may get:
Error: Database connection failed
Full exception
log.error("Error occurred", ex);
You get:
Error occurred

java.sql.SQLException: Database connection failed
    at ...
    at ...
    at ...
For debugging unexpected problems, the second one is much more useful.

A practical approach
You can follow this simple rule:
Expected business exceptions
log.warn("Customer not found with ID: {}", id);
Examples:
Resource not found
Duplicate customer
Invalid business operation
Unexpected system exceptions
log.error("Unexpected error occurred", ex);
Examples:
Database failure
Unexpected NullPointerException
Server failure

Avoid duplicate logging
Consider this:
try {

    customerRepository.save(customer);

} catch (Exception e) {

    log.error("Save failed", e);

    throw e;
}
Then your global exception handler does:
log.error("Unexpected error", e);
Now the same exception may be logged twice.
ERROR Save failed
Stack trace...

ERROR Unexpected error
Stack trace...
Usually, it is better to decide one main place to log the full unexpected exception.
For a simple Spring Boot application, a good approach is:
Service
  ↓
Throw exception
  ↓
Global Exception Handler
  ↓
log.error(..., ex)

Simple flow to remember
Controller
   ↓
Service
   ↓
Exception occurs
   ↓
Throw exception
   ↓
@RestControllerAdvice
   ↓
Log the exception
   ↓
Return ErrorResponse
