Logging to a file — instead of seeing logs only in the IntelliJ console, we'll configure Spring Boot to save logs into a .log file.
10. Logging to a File in Spring Boot
Until now, our logs appeared in the console:
INFO  Customer created successfully
WARN  Customer not found
ERROR Failed to save customer
But when the application stops, console logs are not a convenient permanent record.
We can configure Spring Boot to write logs to a file.

1. Create a log file
In application.properties, add:
logging.file.name=logs/application.log
Now Spring Boot will create:
your-project
│
├── logs
│   └── application.log
│
└── src
Your logs will go to:
application.log

2. Example
Suppose your code contains:
@Slf4j
@Service
public class CustomerService {

    public void createCustomer() {

        log.info("Customer creation started");

        log.info("Customer created successfully");
    }
}
The logs can appear in:
logs/application.log
Example:
INFO  Customer creation started
INFO  Customer created successfully

Console + File
When you configure:
logging.file.name=logs/application.log
Spring Boot can continue logging to the console while also writing logs to the file.
So:
Application
     ↓
   Logback
   ↙     ↘
Console   application.log

3. Configure the file location
You can also specify a different path:
logging.file.name=C:/logs/application.log
Then the file will be created at that location.
Or, commonly inside your project:
logging.file.name=logs/application.log

4. Configure only a directory
You can also configure a directory:
logging.file.path=logs
Then Spring Boot creates its default log file inside that directory.
For learning, I recommend using:
logging.file.name=logs/application.log
because the exact filename is clear.

5. Combine it with log levels
You can configure both together:
logging.level.root=INFO

logging.level.com.example.cruds=DEBUG

logging.file.name=logs/application.log
Meaning:
Spring Boot logs → INFO and above

Your application logs → DEBUG and above

Logs → Console + application.log
For example:
log.debug("Customer ID: {}", id);

log.info("Fetching customer");

log.warn("Customer not found");

log.error("Database error occurred", exception);
These logs can now be stored in:
logs/application.log

Simple flow
Your Code
    ↓
log.info()
log.debug()
log.error()
    ↓
SLF4J
    ↓
Logback
    ↓
┌───────────────┐
│ Console       │
│               │
│ Log File      │
└───────────────┘
Important point
Logging to a file is useful because when an error happens in production, you can inspect the log file and see what happened.
