11. Log Patterns in Spring Boot
When you run a Spring Boot application, you may see logs like:
2026-08-27T10:30:15.123+05:30  INFO 12345 --- [main] c.e.cruds.CrudsApplication : Application started
Let's break this into parts.
2026-08-27T10:30:15.123+05:30
This is the date and time when the log was generated.

INFO
This is the log level.
It can be:
TRACE
DEBUG
INFO
WARN
ERROR

12345
This is the process ID (PID) of the running application.

[main]
This is the thread name.
For example:
[main]
[nio-8080-exec-1]
When an HTTP request is processed, you may see:
[nio-8080-exec-1]
That means a Tomcat request-processing thread handled the request.

c.e.cruds.CrudsApplication
This represents the class/logger name that generated the log.

Application started
This is your actual log message.

Complete structure
DATE + TIME
     ↓
2026-08-27 10:30:15

LOG LEVEL
     ↓
INFO

THREAD
     ↓
[main]

CLASS
     ↓
CustomerService

MESSAGE
     ↓
Customer created successfully
So conceptually:
TIME | LEVEL | THREAD | CLASS | MESSAGE

Customizing the Console Log Pattern
In application.properties, you can customize the format:
logging.pattern.console=%d{yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss} - %level - %logger{36} - %msg%n
This means:
%d       → Date and time

%level   → Log level

%logger  → Logger/class name

%msg     → Actual message

%n       → New line
Example output:
2026-08-27 10:30:15 - INFO - CustomerService - Customer created successfully

Customizing the File Log Pattern
If you're logging to a file:
logging.file.name=logs/application.log
you can configure its pattern separately:
logging.pattern.file=%d{yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss} - %level - %logger - %msg%n
Now the log file may contain:
2026-08-27 10:30:15 - INFO - CustomerService - Customer created successfully
2026-08-27 10:31:20 - WARN - CustomerService - Customer not found
2026-08-27 10:32:10 - ERROR - CustomerService - Database connection failed

Simple configuration for practice
You can currently keep this in your application.properties:
logging.level.root=INFO

logging.level.com.example.cruds=DEBUG

logging.file.name=logs/application.log

logging.pattern.console=%d{yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss} - %level - %logger{36} - %msg%n

logging.pattern.file=%d{yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss} - %level - %logger - %msg%n

One important point
For normal applications, you usually don't need to customize the log pattern immediately. Spring Boot's default pattern is already useful.
Log pattern customization becomes more useful when you want:
cleaner logs
consistent production logs
easier debugging
specific information such as timestamps or thread names
