13. Different Log Levels for Different Packages
In a real Spring Boot application, you may want:
Your code → DEBUG
Spring → INFO
Hibernate → WARN
You can configure this in application.properties.

Example
Suppose your application package is:
com.example.cruds
Configure:
logging.level.root=INFO

logging.level.com.example.cruds=DEBUG
This means:
Spring Boot and other libraries → INFO
Your application                → DEBUG
So your code:
log.debug("Customer ID: {}", id);
will appear.

Controlling Spring Logs
Suppose Spring is producing too many logs.
You can configure:
logging.level.org.springframework=WARN
Now only:
WARN
ERROR
logs from Spring will appear.
Your complete configuration can be:
logging.level.root=INFO

logging.level.com.example.cruds=DEBUG

logging.level.org.springframework=WARN

Controlling Hibernate Logs
Hibernate can also produce many logs.
For example:
logging.level.org.hibernate=WARN
This reduces Hibernate logging.
Or, during debugging:
logging.level.org.hibernate=DEBUG
But be careful—this can produce a lot of output.

Practical Configuration
For your Spring Boot project, something like this is useful:
# General application logging
logging.level.root=INFO

# My application
logging.level.com.example.cruds=DEBUG

# Reduce framework logs
logging.level.org.springframework=WARN
logging.level.org.hibernate=WARN
Now the console is easier to read.
Your application
    ↓
DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR

Spring
    ↓
WARN, ERROR

Hibernate
    ↓
WARN, ERROR

Important concept: Package hierarchy
Suppose you configure:
logging.level.com.example.cruds=DEBUG
Then it also applies to subpackages:
com.example.cruds.controller
com.example.cruds.service
com.example.cruds.repository
com.example.cruds.exception
So you don't need to configure every class separately.

You can configure a specific class too
For example:
logging.level.com.example.cruds.service.CustomerService=TRACE
Now only CustomerService can show TRACE logs.
Example:
log.trace("Entering getCustomer()");
log.debug("Customer ID: {}", id);
log.info("Customer fetched");
All three can be displayed for that specific class.

Simple rule
A good development configuration is:
logging.level.root=INFO
logging.level.com.example.cruds=DEBUG
For production, you will usually keep logging less verbose, for example:
logging.level.root=INFO
logging.level.com.example.cruds=INFO
Enable detailed logs only where you need them.
