14. Logging SQL Queries in Spring Boot and Hibernate
When using JPA/Hibernate, you may want to see the SQL generated by Hibernate.
For example, when you write:
customerRepository.findById(10L);
Hibernate may generate SQL like:
select
    c1_0.customer_id,
    c1_0.name,
    c1_0.email
from customer c1_0
where c1_0.customer_id=?

1. Show SQL
In application.properties:
spring.jpa.show-sql=true
Now Hibernate SQL will be displayed in the console.
Example:
Hibernate:
    select
        c1_0.customer_id,
        c1_0.name
    from
        customer c1_0
    where
        c1_0.customer_id=?
The ? represents a parameter value.
For example:
customerRepository.findById(10L);
The SQL shows:
where customer_id=?
But you may also want to see that the value is 10.

2. Enable Hibernate SQL Logging
You can configure:
logging.level.org.hibernate.SQL=DEBUG
This logs the generated SQL.
Example:
DEBUG org.hibernate.SQL -
select * from customer where customer_id=?

3. Show Query Parameter Values
With modern Hibernate versions, parameter binding can be logged using:
logging.level.org.hibernate.orm.jdbc.bind=TRACE
Now you can see something similar to:
Hibernate SQL:
select * from customer where customer_id=?

Binding parameter [1] as [BIGINT] - [10]
So Hibernate tells you:
? → 10

Recommended Configuration for Development
For your Spring Boot project:
# Show generated SQL
logging.level.org.hibernate.SQL=DEBUG

# Show SQL parameter values
logging.level.org.hibernate.orm.jdbc.bind=TRACE
You can optionally add:
spring.jpa.show-sql=true
But avoid enabling every possible SQL logging option unnecessarily, because you can get duplicate or noisy output.

Complete Example
Suppose you call:
customerRepository.findByEmail("ram@gmail.com");
Your application may log:
INFO  CustomerService - Fetching customer by email: ram@gmail.com

DEBUG org.hibernate.SQL -
select
    c1_0.customer_id,
    c1_0.email,
    c1_0.name
from customer c1_0
where c1_0.email=?

TRACE ... binding parameter [1] - [ram@gmail.com]
The complete flow becomes:
Your Code
    ↓
CustomerRepository
    ↓
Hibernate generates SQL
    ↓
SQL Logger
    ↓
Console / Log File

Important Note
SQL logging is mainly useful during development and debugging.
In production, detailed SQL and parameter logging can:
create huge log files
affect performance
expose sensitive data
So don't blindly enable:
logging.level.org.hibernate.SQL=DEBUG
logging.level.org.hibernate.orm.jdbc.bind=TRACE
in production.

Simple takeaway
# Generated SQL
logging.level.org.hibernate.SQL=DEBUG

# Values passed to ?
logging.level.org.hibernate.orm.jdbc.bind=TRACE
Now you know how to see both:
SQL query
and:
parameter values
